  {
    "episode_id": 79438,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/9/79438.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, I guess what I'd say is I don't think the Senate is fully dead yet, but it is on life support because of the move away from deliberation. Deliberation is what separated the Senate and continues to be something that can separate the Senate from the House of Representatives. And the Senate has always been the body that looks over what's been done in the House, which is purely a partisan body in so many ways, and it's so unfortunate. But it's designed to have a simple majority, and that's what happens there. So there are some people who've been pushing to get the Senate to be just a mirror image of the House of Representatives so that it would go have all of the votes on a simple majority 51 or majority basis. Big mistake because it removes the need and also the requirement for deliberation which the filibuster is in place to secure.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The filibuster exists to protect and ensure deliberation in the Senate.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The House operates under a simple majority rule.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The Senate is not completely dead.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Deliberation distinguishes the Senate from other legislative bodies.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Deliberation is essential to maintaining the Senate's unique role compared to the House of Representatives.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The Senate is currently on life support due to reduced deliberation.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Making the Senate operate by simple majority would eliminate the necessity and requirement for deliberation.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The House of Representatives is primarily a partisan body.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Some individuals advocate making the Senate identical to the House in structure and voting rules.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The filibuster plays a necessary structural role in preserving institutional stability.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Deliberation requires time, discussion, and consensus-building rather than quick decision-making.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The current decline in deliberation threatens the Senate’s legitimacy and function.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "A functional Senate depends on its ability to serve as a check on the more partisan House.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Institutional design should prioritize thoughtful governance over rapid partisan action.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a shared understanding among political actors that the Senate has a distinct constitutional purpose.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The public values the Senate as a deliberative institution and expects it to uphold certain standards.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Changing the Senate to mirror the House would undermine democratic quality by reducing accountability through debate.",
        "confidence": 0.87
