        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Many citizens believe that trust has been replaced by loyalty in politics.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Senators who do not align with their party are seen as problematic.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Genuine trust is necessary for effective negotiations.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Loyalty to political parties undermines authentic trust.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Citizens have a shared perception about the decline of trust in political institutions.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "A senator’s value is partially determined by their adherence to party lines.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker expects the listener to recognize the tension between loyalty and trust.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Agree / Align"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 79438,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/9/79438.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "I agree with that. If you deal, that it's a good deal that is going to last is one where there's mutual trust. You have to trust one another. Even what I've said is if you trust one another, it's only in the case of Russia that you have to verify. President Reagan was spot on. There are times when you try to have trust, but if you have verification, it's not true trust. So that when you work across the aisle to get a partnership going, what's called a gang together, there's mutual trust that's involved with it. But when that's gone, when it's all about party loyalty, and that's what we're seeing today, party loyalty on both sides, believe me, that it becomes very difficult to have the kind of deliberation that we've had in the past because you just, it's disloyal if you cross the aisle. There's a price that you'll pay if you try to go across the aisle and put together a relationship with somebody the other side. I'm not exaggerating it. I think if you talk to others and one of the things I tried to do in the book is we interviewed a number of my former colleagues and those who've left the Senate as well as some who are still in the Senate to get their take on",
